// 소스를 연 채로 인용을 적는다. 요약이 아니라 원문과 그 위치다.
{"id": "e1", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말, 제목 아래 설명)", "quote": "When building agents in Claude Platform, using fewer subagents in favor of progressively disclosed context and tools can improve quality while reducing cost and latency.", "why": "원문의 한 줄 요지"}
{"id": "e2", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "Subagents – agents with their own context window, tools, and instructions, orchestrated by a coordinator – are a core component of agent design. They are effective at verification, parallel work, and modular permissioning.", "why": "원문이 subagent 를 정의하고 쓸모를 인정하는 문장"}
{"id": "e3", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "However, recently the Applied AI team has found a pattern of enterprise agent deployments that have benefited from folding routers, planners, and domain subagents into the main agent loop.", "why": "누가 무엇을 관찰했는가: Applied AI 팀, 기업 배포, router·planner·domain subagent"}
{"id": "e4", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "In our comparisons across several enterprise deployments, a single agent with skills frequently outperformed both the one-prompt-for-everything design and the subagent design on quality, and often at a lower cost and latency per task.", "why": "핵심 비교 주장. 숫자가 없다"}
{"id": "e5", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "For example, one company found the dozen of specialist subagents within its customer-facing agent made a consistent on-brand experience difficult to maintain and added latency that hurt the user experience.", "why": "사례 하나: 전문 subagent 12개, 목소리와 지연"}
{"id": "e6", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "We also often see proposed agent designs that follow a familiar progression: an intent classifier, a fixed multi-step reasoning pipeline, user-segment buckets, separate recommendation branches, and a multi-agent orchestrator on top.", "why": "흔한 설계의 다섯 층"}
{"id": "e7", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "In these cases feedback is often that most of this structure was doing work the model no longer needed done for it, and that one clear system prompt, 4-5 thin data tools, and firm output guardrails would cover most of the use case.", "why": "다섯 층이 모델이 더는 필요 없는 일을 하고 있었다는 판단"}
{"id": "e8", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "A single agent loop hits measurable performance degradation from context bloat, or", "why": "subagent 를 쓸 조건 1"}
{"id": "e9", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "Multiple tasks need to be tackled in parallel with fresh context windows that can be discarded once the task is done, or", "why": "subagent 를 쓸 조건 2"}
{"id": "e10", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "A single agent struggles to select appropriate tools or there are conflicting instructions in the system prompt.", "why": "subagent 를 쓸 조건 3"}
{"id": "e11", "source": "w1", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "But too often we see enterprises give each team or problem type its own subagent, with the agent architecture becoming a copy of their org chart (Conway’s law at work).", "why": "Conway's law: 조직도를 닮은 agent 구조"}
{"id": "e12", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Agent building failure modes for large organizations", "quote": "Typically, each capability is owned by a separate team, so companies start by letting each individual team build their own agent, then a central platform team builds an orchestrator on top.", "why": "큰 조직이 처음 고르는 구조"}
{"id": "e13", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Agent building failure modes for large organizations", "quote": "Context management: You need to decide what context is exchanged between agents and often lose details in the back and forth.", "why": "실패 1"}
{"id": "e14", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Agent building failure modes for large organizations", "quote": "Subagent sprawl: Every team ships its own subagent, so companies end up with hundreds of overlapping subagents with no enforced standards and no specific owner.", "why": "실패 2"}
{"id": "e15", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Agent building failure modes for large organizations", "quote": "The Frankenprompt: Every team adds a sentence to the system prompt, resulting in conflicting rules and no consistent voice.", "why": "실패 3"}
{"id": "e16", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Agent building failure modes for large organizations", "quote": "Unvetted, overly localized evals: Everyone adds their own evals that are overly optimized for their subtask. No one owns or maintains the whole set, and leadership makes decisions based on watered-down, overly localized scores.", "why": "실패 4"}
{"id": "e17", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Agent building failure modes for large organizations", "quote": "One customer that evaluated their multi-agent harness against a single agent loop saw a roughly 2x faster runtime with the latter.", "why": "실패 5 의 유일한 숫자: 고객 한 곳, 약 2배"}
{"id": "e18", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "In other words, the agent sees short descriptions of the skills and tools, which are fully loaded only when needed.", "why": "progressive disclosure 정의"}
{"id": "e19", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "An agent that has seen the result of each earlier step can connect them, which leads to richer outputs and better insights than a set of summaries handed back by subagents. It also means fewer context windows and fewer lossy handoffs.", "why": "이유 1: 공유 context"}
{"id": "e20", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "This removes the main reason teams previously split work across subagents. In fact, we reduced Claude Code’s system prompt for more advanced models by over 80% which included cuts related to progressively disclosing skills and tools.", "why": "이유 2: 나누던 주된 이유가 사라진다. 80%"}
{"id": "e21", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "Models are more capable: starting with Opus 4.5, models became capable of managing far more in a single loop than most teams expected.", "why": "이유 3: Opus 4.5 부터"}
{"id": "e22", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "You cannot turn off thinking on Opus 5.5, and Fable 5.1, and recent models increasingly narrate their progress and check their work as they go, which makes some harness elements redundant.", "why": "하네스 요소가 중복이 되는 이유"}
{"id": "e23", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "Each new model lets you move more of the decision-making out of the harness and into the model itself.", "why": "하네스에서 모델로 판단을 옮긴다: 원문이 말하는 방향"}
{"id": "e24", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "A simpler loop is also easier to move to the next model; in migrations we have helped customers with, rigid multi-agent structures were often what stopped the new model from doing more of the work.", "why": "경직된 구조가 새 모델을 막는다"}
{"id": "e25", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "The single agent loop design requires discipline: a carefully managed system prompt, skill and tool descriptions that trigger at the right times, and deliberate handling of context windows, memory, and note-taking.", "why": "단일 루프의 비용: 규율"}
{"id": "e26", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "One central team should own end-to-end quality and the system prompt, vetting every new tool and eval before it ships. Keeping that central team small helps iteration move quickly and lowers coordination cost across a large org.", "why": "소유 구조: 작은 중앙 팀"}
{"id": "e27", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "If you go the multi-agent route, start with the simplest pattern (generator-verifier, orchestrator-subagent, or other) that could work. Decompose boundaries effectively by dividing by context:", "why": "multi-agent 를 고를 때의 원칙: 가장 단순한 패턴, context 로 나누기"}
{"id": "e28", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Using an agent loop with progressive disclosure", "quote": "When agents are split by problem type, they engage in a \"telephone game,\" passing information back and forth with each handoff degrading fidelity.", "why": "문제 유형으로 나누면 telephone game"}
{"id": "e29", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How to fold subagents into the main agent loop", "quote": "One model and one context window running an open-ended tool loop, with limits (turn and cost budgets, write gates) set in the harness.", "why": "단일 루프 설계 1"}
{"id": "e30", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How to fold subagents into the main agent loop", "quote": "Each turn, the model emits a batch of tool calls. The harness runs them in parallel and returns raw results to the same model, which decides the next step: another batch, a question to the user, or an answer.", "why": "단일 루프 설계 2"}
{"id": "e31", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How to fold subagents into the main agent loop", "quote": "The loop stops when the model answers or hits a budget. A goal or verifier check can optionally run after the answer.", "why": "단일 루프 설계 3"}
{"id": "e32", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How to fold subagents into the main agent loop", "quote": "This is the main change from the orchestrator and specialist agents setup: the model that writes the plan is also the one carrying it out, so it can change the plan mid-turn.", "why": "계획하는 모델과 실행하는 모델이 같다: 원문이 꼽은 가장 큰 변화"}
{"id": "e33", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How to fold subagents into the main agent loop", "quote": "A code step that rewrites the plan from a catalog of rules is no longer needed, since the model decides each step.", "why": "규칙 목록으로 계획을 고치는 코드가 필요 없다"}
{"id": "e34", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How to fold subagents into the main agent loop", "quote": "Hard limits (validation, approval gates) and anything else that comes up mid-conversation (e.g. memory lookup) move into the tools.", "why": "강한 제한은 tool 로"}
{"id": "e35", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How to fold subagents into the main agent loop", "quote": "Code can still be used to load anything cheap and almost always relevant (e.g. an entity resolution step), and to handle any mechanical parts (payload construction, stream parsing, resuming a clarification) inside tools the loop calls.", "why": "코드가 남는 자리"}
{"id": "e36", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How to fold subagents into the main agent loop", "quote": "Ordering and domain procedure move into skills.", "why": "순서와 절차는 skill 로"}
{"id": "e37", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Task-completion evals", "quote": "The eval structure we’ve seen work is one shared end-to-end set owned by a central team with smaller eval sets shipped with each skill by the team that owns it. Even a set of 10 golden examples is enough to start.", "why": "eval 구조, 10개로 시작"}
{"id": "e38", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Task-completion evals", "quote": "While you still have an orchestrator, you should also track routing accuracy (did it pick the right subagent?). Once a subagent is folded in, check instead whether the agent loaded the right skill or called the right tool.", "why": "routing 정확도 → skill/tool 선택"}
{"id": "e39", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Task-completion evals", "quote": "One approach that has worked for us internally is to record timestamps on important events, so they can be replayed later to run as targeted evals.", "why": "replay 어려운 환경의 방법"}
{"id": "e40", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Combining domain agents", "quote": "Merge specialists that chain on each other's output, touch the same data, or are often called together.", "why": "merge 기준"}
{"id": "e41", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Combining domain agents", "quote": "For example, if a significant share of production traffic is one specialist followed by a second, that is a tightly coupled pair, which makes it a candidate to merge into one domain agent.", "why": "merge 예"}
{"id": "e42", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Subagents called as tools", "quote": "We’d only recommend a full handoff, where the subagent takes over the conversation, when it needs to talk to the user directly. Everything else can be a skill plus tools in the main loop.", "why": "handoff 는 사용자와 직접 말할 때만"}
{"id": "e43", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Subagents called as tools", "quote": "This will allow you to define a shared response schema for subagents, verify the conclusion against real data with the main agent, and trim the input to any final synthesis step. This is a good first step, as you don’t need to rewrite the subagents.", "why": "as-tool 의 이점"}
{"id": "e44", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Subagents called as tools", "quote": "Each call still hands back a lossy summary", "why": "as-tool 로도 남는 실패"}
{"id": "e45", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Subagents called as tools", "quote": "Every call is a nested model loop that adds latency", "why": "as-tool 로도 남는 실패"}
{"id": "e46", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Folding subagents into the main agent via skills and tools", "quote": "Fold subagents into the main agent one at a time by turning each one’s prompts into skills and API calls into MCP tools. Compare eval performance with and without the subagent. Then decide which to keep.", "why": "fold 절차"}
{"id": "e47", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Folding subagents into the main agent via skills and tools", "quote": "When we tested this on a sample support agent, folding one subagent into another lowered latency, but also broke a process that had been passing. The main agent was holding the order lookup tool and answering on its own, without asking the shipping specialist for the delivery date.", "why": "원문의 사례: 지연은 줄고 통과하던 과정이 깨짐"}
{"id": "e48", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Folding subagents into the main agent via skills and tools", "quote": "Before each change, make sure your eval has a task for every chain of agents that runs together in production and for every rule the agent must not break, and run each task more than once, since a break like this one only showed up in one run out of two.", "why": "두 번 중 한 번만 드러난 깨짐"}
{"id": "e49", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How several teams can contribute to a single agent", "quote": "Individual teams can shift their ownership from agents to skills and tools, with one small central team keeping ownership of the system prompt and end-to-end quality.", "why": "팀의 소유 단위가 agent 에서 skill·tool 로"}
{"id": "e50", "source": "w1", "locator": "§MCP tools", "quote": "We recommend tools grouped by user intent (not one tool per API) because once you exceed 30-50 tool definitions in the system prompt, tool choice tends to degrade.", "why": "30-50 개 한계, 의도별 묶음"}
{"id": "e51", "source": "w1", "locator": "§MCP tools", "quote": "Each tool checks what the calling user may see or do and returns a plain refusal the model can read, so one loop can serve users with different access without a separate agent per permission level.", "why": "권한도 tool 안에서"}
{"id": "e52", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Skills", "quote": "Skills are the right fit when the agent needs to know how a team does work, like the steps, rules, and judgment calls a specialist subagent's prompt used to hold.", "why": "skill 이 맡는 것"}
{"id": "e53", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Skills", "quote": "To prevent skill sprawl, you can have DRIs per team own their skills and make sure they are well crafted, distinct, and shipped with appropriate evals.", "why": "skill sprawl 방지"}
{"id": "e54", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Knowledge bases", "quote": "Facts and reference material (policies, metric definitions, product specs) can sit behind a search or lookup tool the model calls, or ship as reference files inside a skill. Avoid having code retrieve and paste this context into prompts.", "why": "knowledge base: 코드가 붙여 넣지 않는다"}
{"id": "e55", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Tools to accelerate this work", "quote": "/claude-api prompt-audit: can flag instructions written for older models in your prompts, skills, and tool descriptions and propose fixes as a diff.", "why": "예전 모델용 지시를 찾는 도구"}
{"id": "e56", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Tools to accelerate this work", "quote": "That makes any one architecture less important than the principle behind it: measure, change one thing at a time, and keep what holds up.", "why": "원문의 결론 원칙"}
{"id": "e57", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Tools to accelerate this work", "quote": "Build a small end-to-end eval, fold in one subagent, and keep it as a subagent only if the folded version scores worse, then rinse and repeat.", "why": "원문의 마지막 지시"}
{"id": "e58", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Tools to accelerate this work", "quote": "As models get more capable and agents get longer running, more of your subagents can be skills and tools. You don’t have to make that move in one step, though.", "why": "점진 이행"}
{"id": "e59", "source": "w2", "locator": "§What is a multi-agent system?", "quote": "Today, multi-agent systems are often applied in situations where a single agent would perform better, though this calculus continues to evolve as models improve.", "why": "2026-01 에 이미 같은 경고"}
{"id": "e60", "source": "w2", "locator": "§What is a multi-agent system?", "quote": "we've identified three situations where multiple agents consistently outperform a single agent: when context pollution degrades performance, when tasks can run in parallel, and when specialization improves tool selection or task focus.", "why": "2026-01 의 세 상황. w1 의 세 조건과 대응"}
{"id": "e61", "source": "w2", "locator": "§The case for starting with a single agent", "quote": "In our testing, multi-agent implementations typically use 3-10x more tokens than single-agent approaches for equivalent tasks.", "why": "토큰 3-10배"}
{"id": "e62", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Tool set specialization", "quote": "Quantity. An agent with too many tools (often 20+) struggles to select the appropriate one.", "why": "2026-01 의 tool 수 기준 20+"}
{"id": "e63", "source": "w2", "locator": "§System prompt specialization", "quote": "When a single agent must switch between conflicting behavioral modes, separating into specialized agents with tailored system prompts produces more consistent results.", "why": "persona 충돌이면 agent 를 나눈다: 2026-01 의 권고"}
{"id": "e64", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Domain expertise specialization", "quote": "Rather than loading all domain context into a single agent, specialized agents can carry focused expertise relevant to their specific responsibilities.", "why": "domain 지식은 전문 agent 에: 2026-01 의 권고"}
{"id": "e65", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Domain expertise specialization", "quote": "A single agent with 40+ tools often struggles to select correctly, confusing similar operations across platforms. Splitting into specialized agents with focused toolsets and tailored prompts resolves selection errors.", "why": "40+ tool 이면 나눈다: 2026-01 의 예"}
{"id": "e66", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Domain expertise specialization", "quote": "This pattern mirrors effective professional collaboration, where specialists with tools matched to their roles collaborate more effectively than generalists attempting to maintain expertise across all domains.", "why": "전문가 협업 비유: w1 이 Conway's law 로 되받는 논리"}
{"id": "e67", "source": "w2", "locator": "§(single-agent 한계 신호)", "quote": "Before adopting a multi-agent architecture, consider using the Tool Search Tool, which lets Claude dynamically discover tools on-demand rather than loading all definitions upfront. This can reduce token usage by up to 85% while improving tool selection accuracy.", "why": "tool search 는 이미 2026-01 에 권고"}
{"id": "e68", "source": "w2", "locator": "§(single-agent 한계 신호)", "quote": "These thresholds will shift as models improve. Current limits represent practical guidelines, not fundamental constraints.", "why": "기준이 움직인다는 예고"}
{"id": "e69", "source": "w2", "locator": "§(분해 원칙)", "quote": "The key insight is to adopt a context-centric view rather than a problem-centric view when decomposing work.", "why": "context 로 나누기는 2026-01 부터"}
{"id": "e70", "source": "w2", "locator": "§(분해 원칙)", "quote": "In one experiment with agents specialized by software development role (planner, implementer, tester, reviewer), the subagents spent more tokens on coordination than on actual work.", "why": "역할별 분업 실험"}
{"id": "e71", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Verification subagent", "quote": "It's worth noting that more capable orchestrator models (like Claude Opus 4.5) are increasingly able to evaluate subagent work directly without a separate verification step.", "why": "Opus 4.5 의 직접 검증"}
{"id": "e72", "source": "w2", "locator": "§(본문 링크)", "quote": "Outside these situations, the coordination costs typically exceed the benefits. See when to fold subagents back into the main agent loop.", "why": "받은 사본에 w1 로 가는 링크가 들어 있다"}
{"id": "e73", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Getting started", "quote": "For most use cases, we recommend starting with orchestrator-subagent. It handles the widest range of problems with the least coordination overhead.", "why": "2026-04 의 기본 출발점"}
{"id": "e74", "source": "w3", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "We recommend starting with the simplest pattern that could work, watching where it struggles, and evolving from there.", "why": "가장 단순한 패턴부터: w1 과 같은 문장"}
{"id": "e75", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Pattern 4: Message bus", "quote": "This pipeline suits the message bus because events flow from one stage to the next, teams can add new agent types as threat categories evolve, and teams can develop and deploy agents independently.", "why": "팀별 agent 독립 배포를 장점으로 든 2026-04 문장"}
{"id": "e76", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Pattern 2: Orchestrator-subagent", "quote": "The orchestrator becomes an information bottleneck. See when to fold subagents back into the main agent loop.", "why": "받은 사본에 w1 로 가는 링크"}
{"id": "e77", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Workflow: Routing", "quote": "Routing classifies an input and directs it to a specialized followup task. This workflow allows for separation of concerns, and building more specialized prompts.", "why": "2024-12 의 routing 정의"}
{"id": "e78", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Workflow: Routing", "quote": "Directing different types of customer service queries (general questions, refund requests, technical support) into different downstream processes, prompts, and tools.", "why": "routing 의 고객 지원 예: w1 의 support agent 와 같은 영역"}
{"id": "e79", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Workflow: Orchestrator-workers", "quote": "In the orchestrator-workers workflow, a central LLM dynamically breaks down tasks, delegates them to worker LLMs, and synthesizes their results.", "why": "2024-12 의 orchestrator-workers"}
{"id": "e80", "source": "w4", "locator": "§What are agents?", "quote": "Workflows are systems where LLMs and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths.", "why": "workflow 정의"}
{"id": "e81", "source": "w4", "locator": "§When (and when not) to use agents", "quote": "When building applications with LLMs, we recommend finding the simplest solution possible, and only increasing complexity when needed.", "why": "단순함부터: 이어지는 원칙"}
{"id": "e82", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Agents", "quote": "They are typically just LLMs using tools based on environmental feedback in a loop.", "why": "agent = loop 의 정의. w1 의 단일 루프와 같은 뿌리"}
{"id": "e83", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Combining and customizing these patterns", "quote": "To repeat: you should consider adding complexity only when it demonstrably improves outcomes.", "why": "측정해서 더한다: w1 의 결론과 같은 원칙"}
{"id": "e84", "source": "w4", "locator": "§(머리말 안내)", "quote": "Note: Much of the tooling landscape described in this post has changed since December 2024.", "why": "받은 사본은 고쳐진 판"}
{"id": "e85", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Benefits of a multi-agent system", "quote": "We found that a multi-agent system with Claude Opus 4 as the lead agent and Claude Sonnet 4 subagents outperformed single-agent Claude Opus 4 by 90.2% on our internal research eval.", "why": "2025-06 의 90.2%"}
{"id": "e86", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Benefits of a multi-agent system", "quote": "We found that token usage by itself explains 80% of the variance, with the number of tool calls and the model choice as the two other explanatory factors.", "why": "토큰이 분산의 80%"}
{"id": "e87", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Benefits of a multi-agent system", "quote": "In our data, agents typically use about 4× more tokens than chat interactions, and multi-agent systems use about 15× more tokens than chats.", "why": "15배"}
{"id": "e88", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Benefits of a multi-agent system", "quote": "Further, some domains that require all agents to share the same context or involve many dependencies between agents are not a good fit for multi-agent systems today.", "why": "2025-06 에도 공유 context 영역은 예외였다"}
{"id": "e89", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Benefits of a multi-agent system", "quote": "We’ve found that multi-agent systems excel at valuable tasks that involve heavy parallelization, information that exceeds single context windows, and interfacing with numerous complex tools.", "why": "2025-06 의 multi-agent 적합 조건"}
{"id": "e90", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Production reliability and engineering challenges", "quote": "Subagent output to a filesystem to minimize the ‘game of telephone.’", "why": "telephone game 표현은 2025-06 에도 있다"}
{"id": "e91", "source": "w6", "locator": "§Principles of Context Engineering", "quote": "Share context, and share full agent traces, not just individual messages", "why": "Cognition 원칙 1"}
{"id": "e92", "source": "w6", "locator": "§Principles of Context Engineering", "quote": "Actions carry implicit decisions, and conflicting decisions carry bad results", "why": "Cognition 원칙 2"}
{"id": "e93", "source": "w6", "locator": "§Applying the Principles", "quote": "it is evident that in 2025, running multiple agents in collaboration only results in fragile systems. The decision-making ends up being too dispersed and context isn’t able to be shared thoroughly enough between the agents.", "why": "2025-06 Cognition 의 판단"}
{"id": "e94", "source": "w6", "locator": "§Applying the Principles", "quote": "The simplest way to follow the principles is to just use a single-threaded linear agent:", "why": "Cognition 의 대안: 단일 스레드 agent"}
{"id": "e95", "source": "w7", "locator": "§(머리말)", "quote": "We removed over 80% of Claude Code’s system prompt for models like Claude Opus 5 and Claude Fable 5 with no measurable loss on our coding evaluations.", "why": "80% 의 근거와 조건: 코딩 eval"}
{"id": "e96", "source": "w7", "locator": "§Then: Put it all upfront / Now: Use progressive disclosure", "quote": "Some of our tools are ‘deferred loading,’ which means the agent must search for their full definitions using ToolSearch before using them.", "why": "deferred loading"}
{"id": "e97", "source": "w7", "locator": "§Unhobbling Claude", "quote": "And while these constraints were once needed to avoid worst case scenarios, we have since found we can delete many of them and let the model use surrounding context and judgement instead.", "why": "규칙을 지우고 판단에 맡긴다"}
{"id": "e98", "source": "w8", "locator": "§(아키텍처)", "quote": "There is no intent router in front of it that segments the conversation and no set of domain specific agents behind it.", "why": "2026-09 commerce 글: router 도 domain agent 도 없다"}
{"id": "e99", "source": "w8", "locator": "§Skills, not subagents", "quote": "Every handoff to a subagent is a state-lossy operation, which often impacts the quality of the subagent’s response and, consequently, the overall response.", "why": "handoff 는 상태를 잃는다"}
{"id": "e100", "source": "w8", "locator": "§Skills, not subagents", "quote": "In our comparisons across several enterprise deployments, a single agent with skills consistently has outperformed both the one-prompt-for-everything design and the subagent design on quality, and often at a lower cost and latency per task.", "why": "w1 이 링크한 원 문장. consistently → w1 은 frequently"}
{"id": "e101", "source": "w8", "locator": "§Skills, not subagents", "quote": "A good starting point is that anything relevant to a third or more of your traffic, whether anticipated before launch or observed in production, goes in the system prompt, and the rest goes in skills.", "why": "prompt 와 skill 의 경계: 트래픽 1/3"}
{"id": "e102", "source": "w9", "locator": "§Progressive disclosure", "quote": "metadata uses ~50 tokens, full SKILL.md files ~500 tokens, and reference files 2,000+ tokens and only when specifically needed.", "why": "progressive disclosure 의 크기"}
{"id": "e103", "source": "r1", "locator": "README.md:13", "quote": "The kit does not assume a single loop always wins. It measures. Every change is scored on\nthe same eval before and after, and a subagent only goes away when the numbers say its\nreplacement holds.", "why": "kit 의 원칙"}
{"id": "e104", "source": "r1", "locator": "README.md:11", "quote": "> **Reference implementation. Not maintained and not accepting contributions.**", "why": "관리되지 않는 reference"}
{"id": "e105", "source": "r1", "locator": "README.md:30", "quote": "`examples/support-desk` is a small, runnable support agent with a router and six subagents,\nbuilt so that each subagent has a known right answer.", "why": "예제: router 와 subagent 6개"}
{"id": "e106", "source": "r1", "locator": "README.md:139", "quote": "Two runs of the example, by one person on one model, both recorded as they came out in", "why": "실제 모델 실행은 한 사람, 한 모델, 두 번"}
{"id": "e107", "source": "r1", "locator": "README.md:145", "quote": "Not run on a real model: the merge and upkeep steps, the finished `single_loop/` version of\nthe example, tasks t18 and t19, starting from a repo with no eval, and the kit on any\nsystem other than the example.", "why": "실제 모델로 돌리지 않은 것"}
{"id": "e108", "source": "r1", "locator": "skill/steps/00-audit.md:67", "quote": "| `fold` | The default. The subagent is a prompt (procedure, domain knowledge) plus some API calls, and nothing about its work needs a separate context. It becomes a skill, tools, or both, in the main agent. |", "why": "fold 가 기본 판정"}
{"id": "e109", "source": "r1", "locator": "skill/steps/00-audit.md:70", "quote": "| `keep` | It has to hold the conversation with the user itself, or it belongs to another company and cannot be changed. The full handoff stays. This is rare. |", "why": "keep 은 드물다"}
{"id": "e110", "source": "r1", "locator": "skill/steps/00-audit.md:75", "quote": "1. **Context.** It reads far more than the main agent needs afterwards: logs, long\n   documents, big search results. As a rough guide, tens of thousands of tokens per call\n   qualifies, and a few thousand does not.", "why": "context 조건의 크기 기준"}
{"id": "e111", "source": "r1", "locator": "skill/steps/00-audit.md:87", "quote": "- A different team inside the company owns it. Ownership moves to the skill and its eval.\n- It has its own persona or voice. One agent should have one voice.\n- Its prompt is long. A skill's body is only loaded when it is needed.\n- It has many tools. Tool search loads tool definitions on demand.", "why": "따로 context 를 가질 이유가 되지 않는 것"}
{"id": "e112", "source": "r1", "locator": "skill/steps/00-audit.md:91", "quote": "- Its steps are fixed. Fixed steps are a workflow, which is plain code. Give it `fold`: the\n  fixed part goes inside a tool, and whatever still needs judgment (choosing, explaining)\n  goes in a skill.", "why": "고정 단계는 tool 안의 코드로"}
{"id": "e113", "source": "r1", "locator": "skill/steps/00-audit.md:78", "quote": "Before you settle on this, ask whether the tool\n   could simply return less (filter, search, paginate). If it could, the better plan is\n   often `fold` with a better tool.", "why": "tool 이 덜 돌려주면 fold"}
{"id": "e114", "source": "r1", "locator": "reference_loop/loop.py:14", "quote": "  * Hard limits live in the harness, not the prompt: a turn budget, a token\n    budget that also counts what subagents spend, a cap on calls per batch,\n    and a write gate in front of any tool that changes something.", "why": "reference loop 의 하네스 제한"}
{"id": "e115", "source": "r1", "locator": "examples/support-desk/example-run/README.md:7", "quote": "Run on 2026-10-08 with `KIT_MODEL=claude-opus-5`, two reps per variant, no harness errors.", "why": "첫 실행의 조건"}
